---
import Work from "@layouts/Work.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    title: "Rainbow School",
    date: "2020.05.29",
    coverImage: "/rainbowschool/Rainbow School.png",
};
---

<Work
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Journey Through Light, Instinct, and Play"
    description="Rooted in the belief that learning emerges through play, this kindergarten invites children to engage with space through curiosity, challenge, and subconscious exploration. The design began with two conceptual seeds: a blindfolded drawing exercise and the folklore of Red Riding Hood. The resulting drawing revealed a woven rope-like section, inspiring a massing strategy of overlapping cubes that form spatial volumes. The landscape echoes this logic, composed of square platforms and connective slits that encourage movement and discovery."
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio II"
    courseDescription="Learning by Playing or A Kindergartener's Approach to Architecture"
    advisor="Daniel Horowitz"
    collaborators=""
    date={frontmatter.date}
    location=""
    gallery={import.meta.glob("/public/rainbowschool/gallery/*")}
>
    <section class="stack">
        <p>
            Children learn by exploring the limits around them. In early
            childhood, constraints don't restrict imagination. They spark it.
            This kindergarten is built around that idea. The architecture
            creates gentle boundaries, obstacles, and moments of challenge that
            encourage children to notice, explore, and understand their
            surroundings through play.
        </p>
        <p>
            The project draws from three sources: a blindfolded drawing
            exercise, the story structure of Little Red Riding Hood, and the
            artwork Three Figures' geometric compositions. Each introduces a
            different kind of constraint. Together, they form a simple and
            grounded design approach where shapes, colors, and movement become
            tools for discovery.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Concept</h3>
        <p>
            The building treats architecture as a learning partner. Instead of
            teaching through instruction, it teaches through experience.
            Children move through spaces that ask them to crawl, climb, pause,
            and choose. They encounter openings, tight passages, changes in
            scale, and shifting colored light. These moments are small
            constraints that spark curiosity. The building becomes a place where
            learning happens naturally because the environment quietly
            encourages exploration.
        </p>
        <img src="/rainbowschool/Axon NW.jpg" alt="Axon NW" />
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Blindfolded Drawing</h3>
        <p>
            The first inspiration came from a blindfolded drawing exercise. With
            sight removed, the only way to draw was through movement and feel.
            Lines slowed down. Pressure changed. Marks gathered toward the
            center where the hand felt safe. The drawing became a record of
            instinct rather than intention.
        </p>
        <p>
            A square portion of the drawing was selected for closer study.
            Following each line meant deciding which crossing felt like the
            natural continuation. The drawing began to read like a
            three-dimensional system of overlaps and threads. This logic of
            nodes and connections informed the early massing studies. Squares
            interlocked. Boxes overlapped. Simple shapes created complex
            relationships. The constraint of blindness revealed a spatial system
            built from basic geometry.
        </p>

        <div class="row">
            <figure class="stack fit">
                <img
                    src="/rainbowschool/Drawing (Cut).jpg"
                    alt="Blindfolded Drawing"
                />
                <figcaption>Blindfolded drawing</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="stack fit">
                <img
                    src="/rainbowschool/Drawing (Annotated).png"
                    alt="Analysis of Blindfolded Drawing"
                />
                <figcaption>Analysis of blindfolded drawing</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="stack fit">
                <img src="/rainbowschool/2D Study.jpg" alt="2D Study" />
                <figcaption>2D study with paper squares</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="stack fit">
                <img src="/rainbowschool/3D Study.jpg" alt="3D Study" />
                <figcaption>3D study with boxes</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row">
        <figure class="stack fit">
            <h3>Little Red Riding Hood</h3>
            <img
                src="/rainbowschool/Little Red Riding Hood.png"
                alt="Little Red Riding Hood"
            />
            <figcaption class="stack">
                <p>
                    The second inspiration came from analyzing <a
                        href="https://books.google.com/books/about/Little_Red_Riding_Hood.html?id=_HG-6XB92jQC"
                        >Candice Ransom's <cite>Little Red Riding Hood</cite>
                    </a> as a sequence of constraints that shape the protagonist's
                    journey. She is given a cape and adopts it without question. She
                    is told to stay on a path. She is tempted to leave it. She notices
                    something strange. She reacts. She reflects. Her learning comes
                    from the encounters along the way, not from the destination.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This structure translates directly into spatial behavior.
                    Boundaries guide movement. Openings invite detours. Changes
                    in scale sharpen awareness. Obstacles trigger reactions.
                    Quiet corners allow reflection. Children learn by navigating
                    these moments, just as the protagonist learns through her
                    own path.
                </p>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="stack fit">
            <h3>Three Figures</h3>
            <img src="/rainbowschool/Three Figures.jpg" alt="Three Figures" />
            <figcaption>
                The final inspiration came from <a
                    href="https://www.artchive.com/artwork/three-figures-fernand-leger-1921/"
                    >Fernand Léger's <cite>Three Figures</cite>
                </a>. The artwork uses simple shapes and a limited palette.
                These constraints mirror early childhood education, where
                children first learn circles, squares, triangles, and basic
                colors. Using Léger's language, the project adopts clear
                geometry and selective color to organize solid and transparent
                surfaces. This creates an environment that children can read
                intuitively. Shapes stack and overlap. Color filters light and
                animates surfaces throughout the day.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Architectural Translation</h3>
        <p>
            The building expresses <i>Discovery Through Constraint</i> through shapes,
            colors, and movement. Overlapping cubes form the massing. Interlocking
            squares create openings and enclosures. Selective colors applied to glazing
            filter sunlight into shifting patterns that quietly teach lessons in perception.
            Circulation is calibrated to the scale of kindergarteners. Ramps and stairs
            build confidence. Ladders introduce challenges. Tight passages encourage
            crawling. Open platforms invite gathering. Children choose routes based
            on comfort and curiosity. Movement becomes a way of thinking.
        </p>
        <img
            src="/rainbowschool/Program & Section.jpg"
            alt="Program & Section"
        />
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Final Synthesis</h3>
        <p>
            This kindergarten is built on a simple idea. Children learn best
            when the world gives them just enough structure to spark discovery.
            The blindfolded drawing, Little Red Riding Hood, and Three Figures
            each reveal how creativity emerges when freedom meets constraint.
            The architecture adopts this logic, creating a building that guides,
            challenges, and invites exploration. It is a place where children
            learn through play because the environment itself teaches through
            experience.
        </p>
    </section>
</Work>
